1.30.m:
They are remembered by the names Agniṣvāttas and Barhiṣads.
Those among them who did not sacrifice were householders;
those ancestors who maintained no sacrificial fire are remembered as the Agniṣvāttas.

Those among them who were sacrificers were ancestors who drank Soma;
those ancestors who performed the Agnihotra are remembered as the Barhiṣads.
‘The seasons are the ancestors, the gods’—such is the settled conclusion in this treatise.

Iṣa and Ūrja are declared full of nectar;
Sahas and Sahasya are remembered as wrathful;
Tapas and Tapasya, the two winter months, are dreadful.

Menā and Dhāriṇī, by whom this universe is upheld.
For the sake of dharma, the ancestors gave their two auspicious daughters;
they both were exponents of the Veda, and both were yoginīs.

Menā, wife of Himavat, gave birth to Maināka,
and Gaṅgā, best of rivers, who became wife of the salt ocean;
and Maināka's younger brother was Krauñca, after whom Krauñcadvīpa is remembered.

Sāvarṇī, [also called] Sāmudrī, wife of Prācīnabarhis;
that Savarṇā, Sāmudrī, [bore] ten Prācīnabarhis sons,
all named Pracetas, masters of the science of archery.

“Dakṣa had eight daughters, the maidens whom I mentioned.
Having fetched them from their homes, their father honored them at his house;
and then, having been honored, they all dwelt in their father's house.

